Safely Adding a Column to a Massive Production Table with ZERO Downtime

Scenario:
You need to add a new column to a database table containing 300 million rows. The table is being continuously read from and written to by users in a production application running 24/7.
You cannot take the database offline, and you cannot afford any downtime.
How would you safely add the new column without impacting the production application?

The Approach to Avoid:
If your first instinct is to add the column and immediately update all 300 million rows in one giant transaction — stop right there! 

ALTER TABLE users
ADD COLUMN plan TEXT;

UPDATE users
SET plan = 'free';

updating 300,000,000 rows ...

A transaction that large will lock a massive number of rows. 
With those rows locked, your live traffic will have to wait for the lock to be released, inevitably causing the one thing you are trying to avoid: downtime.

Production Approach: Instead of one large migration, break the change into small, safe stages.

Stage 1: Add the New Column as Nullable

First, add the new column as nullable and without a default value.

ALTER TABLE users
ADD COLUMN plan TEXT NULL;

Why Nullable?
For many modern databases, adding a nullable column can be primarily a metadata/schema change, meaning the database does not necessarily need to physically rewrite every existing row just to add the column.

The existing 300 million rows can therefore remain untouched initially.

Conceptually:

Before:

id | name | age
---|------|----
1  | John | 30
2  | Mike | 25


After:

id | name | age | plan
---|------|-----|------
1  | John | 30  | NULL
2  | Mike | 25  | NULL

The new column exists, but historical records have no value yet.

Stage 2: Fix New Writes First

This is a critical part of the migration.

The application is continuously receiving new requests:

|--------------------------------------|
Users → Application → Database
             ↑
          24/7 writes
|--------------------------------------|

If you start backfilling old rows before fixing new writes, new records may continue to arrive with:

plan = NULL

So the backlog can continue growing.

Solution: Update the application logic first.

The new application should populate the new column whenever a new record is created.
|--------------------------------------|
New Application
      ↓
name + age + plan
      ↓
Database
|--------------------------------------|

For example:

INSERT INTO users (name, age, plan)
VALUES ('John', 30, 'free');

Then deploy the new application.

After deployment:
|--------------------------------------|
OLD DATA
300M existing rows
        ↓
   plan may be NULL


NEW DATA
New rows
        ↓
   plan populated
|--------------------------------------|
Now the migration only needs to deal with the historical backlog.

Stage 3: Backfill Old Data

Now populate the new column for existing records.

Don't do:
UPDATE users
SET plan = 'free';

for all 300 million rows in one transaction.

Use Batch Processing: Run a background job that fills the new column for historical rows in small batches (e.g., a few thousand rows at a time). 
|--------------------------------------|
300 Million Rows
       ↓
Batch 1 → Few thousand
       ↓
Batch 2 → Few thousand
       ↓
Batch 3 → Few thousand
       ↓
...
       ↓
All historical rows updated
|--------------------------------------|

Example:

UPDATE users
SET plan = 'free'
WHERE id >= 1
  AND id < 5000
  AND plan IS NULL;

Then process the next batch.

After each batch, briefly pause and check your database load. If the load gets too high, pause the processing and wait for it to stabilize before starting the next batch.
